\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Real positivity and the residue at eleven}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{For real #{t=\tau}, the four eigenvalues of #{Q(\tau)} are
positive exactly when #{\tau>-1/2}. The running weighted graph
assumes the smaller domain #{\tau>0}. At #{t=1} modulo #{11},
the same point of #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}} disappears after inverting
#{11}, while every real specialization survives that inversion.}

\proof{
\p{The matrix #{H/2} from \ref{fdip-000U} is orthogonal, and
the four eigenvalues of #{Q(\tau)} are
#{1,3,2\tau+1,2\tau+3}. They are all positive exactly when
#{\tau>-1/2}. Each such evaluation sends #{2,\kappa,s,f} to
nonzero reals and extends uniquely to
##{R_{\mathrm{obs}}\longrightarrow\mathbb R,\qquad t\longmapsto\tau.}
It gives #{\Spec\mathbb R\to X_{\mathrm{obs}}}
and recovers #{Q(\tau),S(\tau),P,J,R(\tau)}. The
[Clifford base-change isomorphism](fdip-000V) transports their
full-form algebra and symmetry; it does not require evaluation
to be injective. Gaussian integration and the
[normalized marginal](fdip-000E) apply on this positive real
locus.}

\p{At #{t=1} modulo #{11}, #{\kappa=45}, #{s=3}, and #{f=2}
are all nonzero. The point therefore lies in #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}},
but disappears after further inverting #{11}. Every real
specialization survives that extra inversion. Thus the same
real family admits different arithmetic opens.}
}
